WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.360
Some of you may be thinking, why do you need state management?

00:03.360 --> 00:06.640
After all, you have the power of composables.

00:06.640 --> 00:08.320
Who needs it anymore?

00:08.320 --> 00:11.920
I can do everything. We don't need state management stores.

00:11.920 --> 00:14.960
Yes, it is true. We basically model how data source work,

00:14.960 --> 00:17.200
you basically are doing your state, your getters, your actions.

00:18.160 --> 00:20.960
The thing I always tell people about state management libraries

00:20.960 --> 00:24.640
is that what you're providing your team is a set of standards that everyone can follow.

00:25.360 --> 00:29.520
Now granted, if you're going to author your own internal state management library

00:29.520 --> 00:33.600
that you heavily document and that's type-save and if you want to do all that,

00:33.600 --> 00:36.560
that is awesome and I like more power to you.

00:37.360 --> 00:40.160
But one of the things that things like Piniad does allow us to do

00:40.720 --> 00:43.840
is the fact that you have these full-time open source maintainers

00:44.400 --> 00:49.040
who are basically trying to create the, especially within the view community,

00:49.040 --> 00:52.800
they're very motivated to create the best UX they can give you.

00:52.800 --> 00:55.440
And so, for example, you can see here in the new DevTools,

00:56.080 --> 01:00.320
let's see, here's like the little Piniad icon, what pineapple.

01:01.040 --> 01:04.240
You can see here, you can actually see all the stuff, you can inspect it,

01:04.240 --> 01:08.000
you can even modify it, if you need to test anything, save it down.

01:08.960 --> 01:12.640
It can even track the timeline in terms of mutations. So, I believe

01:13.600 --> 01:17.200
if I went in here and changed this,

01:18.480 --> 01:20.720
yeah, I guess you were quite up big task.

01:20.800 --> 01:26.000
Oh, oh, I think I didn't know why, that one's not connected yet.

01:26.560 --> 01:28.480
But you can see here, basically, there,

01:30.240 --> 01:31.360
you get a lot of this work for free.

01:32.240 --> 01:35.600
And to be honest, if you look underneath Piniad in particular,

01:36.160 --> 01:39.840
it's really not that far from composable. It's just a set of standards that's kind of been

01:39.840 --> 01:43.360
agreed upon by the community. So, if you're worried about like bulk in that kind of stuff,

01:44.160 --> 01:48.080
Piniad has already, unlike for those of us who were in the UX days, the view too,

01:48.720 --> 01:51.440
that was heavier. Because a lot of times,

01:51.440 --> 01:54.480
you had to do a lot to manually optimize the performance of it.

01:54.480 --> 01:58.160
So, back then, a lot of people would use global stores, basically, one big store,

01:58.160 --> 02:01.520
and they would chunk out small stores. But what they didn't know was that all the

02:01.520 --> 02:04.800
sorts of being lumped in together. So, anytime someone loaded something, they were loading.

02:04.800 --> 02:08.160
It was the whole like, assorably, and I got to get the rainforest kind of thing.

02:08.880 --> 02:11.600
And so, Piniad, by default, every time we do something,

02:12.480 --> 02:16.240
like we see here where we just do like a test store, time entry store,

02:16.240 --> 02:20.080
those are already tree shaken, they're separate. They're basically equivalent of these

02:20.080 --> 02:24.240
composables we have here where as long as you're importing them on specific files,

02:24.240 --> 02:29.040
like it's already doing that for you. There's no like giant glob that's being built together

02:29.040 --> 02:34.720
and then being shipped through users. And so, for that reason, I find that generally speaking,

02:34.720 --> 02:40.080
state management libraries are helpful for especially large teams. Teams that need some standards.

